## Recommended: Adopt `System.IO.Abstractions`

This is the established NuGet package for wrapping `File.*` and `Directory.*` statics — no need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs or Startup.cs:
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string ReadConfig(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void SaveOutput(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.ReadConfig("/data/config.json"));

service.SaveOutput("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));
```

### Why this over a custom wrapper?

| Concern | `System.IO.Abstractions` | Custom `IFileService` |
|---------|--------------------------|----------------------|
| Coverage | Full `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream` API | Only what you wrap |
| Maintenance | Community-maintained | You own it |
| Test helpers | `MockFileSystem` included | Roll your own mocks |
| Adoption cost | One NuGet install | Write + maintain code |

---

Once you've registered `IFileSystem` in DI, the next step is replacing the existing `File.*`/`Directory.*` call sites with the injected instance. If you'd like help with that bulk migration, let me know and I can walk you through it.
